---
import '../styles/evidence-operation.css';
---

<section id="evidence" class="evidence-layer shell" aria-labelledby="evidence-title">
  <div class="evidence-heading evidence-reveal">
    <div>
      <span class="evidence-index">03 / EVIDENCE & OPERATION</span>
      <h2 id="evidence-title">Design claims are cheap.<br />Operational evidence is not.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      The previous layer explains why these systems exist. This layer connects those claims to code paths, failure cases,
      regression history and the infrastructure that still has to run.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="evidence-trace evidence-reveal" aria-hidden="true">
    <span>design claim</span><i></i><span>implementation</span><i></i><span>failure</span><i></i><span>operation</span>
  </div>

  <article class="evidence-system evidence-reveal" id="evidence-minecartainer">
    <header class="evidence-system-header">
      <div>
        <span class="evidence-kind">STATEFUL RUNTIME</span>
        <h3>Minecartainer</h3>
      </div>
      <p>Persistent-state safety is encoded in files, startup policy, shutdown behavior and executable failure paths.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="evidence-grid">
      <a class="evidence-record evidence-record-primary" href="https://github.com/AlexanderGG-0520/minecartainer/blob/main/docs/server-install-reinstall-policy.md">
        <span class="record-type">PERSISTED STATE</span>
        <code>/data/.server-install.json</code>
        <strong>Managed install identity survives container replacement.</strong>
        <p>
          artifact / type / version / build are persisted with the world-facing volume so a new container does not guess
          what already exists.
        </p>
        <small>source → server install / reinstall policy ↗</small>
      </a>

      <a class="evidence-record" href="https://github.com/AlexanderGG-0520/minecartainer/blob/main/scripts/lib/install.sh">
        <span class="record-type">IMPLEMENTATION</span>
        <code>install()</code>
        <strong>Configuration drift is rejected before runtime begins.</strong>
        <p>
          A valid managed marker that disagrees with requested server identity fails by default. Reinstallation requires
          explicit intent instead of silently mutating durable state.
        </p>
        <small>source → scripts/lib/install.sh ↗</small>
      </a>

      <a class="evidence-record" href="https://github.com/AlexanderGG-0520/minecartainer/blob/main/.github/workflows/runtime-smoke.yml">
        <span class="record-type">EXECUTABLE CONTRACT</span>
        <code>runtime-smoke.yml</code>
        <strong>Failure paths are tested as part of the product contract.</strong>
        <p>
          Runtime CI covers invalid state and lifecycle behavior instead of treating only the happy-path server launch as
          success.
        </p>
        <small>source → GitHub Actions workflow ↗</small>
      </a>

      <div class="evidence-record evidence-record-observed">
        <span class="record-type">OPERATION</span>
        <code>Kubernetes / PVC</code>
        <strong>Single-writer state and graceful termination shape deployment policy.</strong>
        <p>
          A disposable Pod can be recreated; the world cannot. Recreate strategy, readiness, RCON save/stop and sufficient
          termination grace make that asymmetry explicit in operation.
        </p>
        <small>observed runtime policy</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="evidence-system evidence-reveal" id="evidence-mc-router">
    <header class="evidence-system-header">
      <div>
        <span class="evidence-kind">NETWORK EDGE</span>
        <h3>mc-router</h3>
      </div>
      <p>The strongest evidence for a responsibility boundary is the production regression that crossed it.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="incident-record" data-incident-record>
      <div class="incident-meta">
        <span>INCIDENT / RECOVERY</span>
        <code>36c75dc</code>
        <time datetime="2026-08-24">2026-08-24</time>
      </div>

      <div class="incident-copy">
        <span class="incident-state">STATUS ROUTING REGRESSION</span>
        <h4>Router-owned monitoring state had begun to influence player-facing STATUS semantics.</h4>
        <p>
          The recovery restored explicit STATUS precedence, kept LOGIN / Transfer on the normal backend, removed monitored
          availability as an implicit response authority and expanded structured observability around route selection.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/AlexanderGG-0520/mc-router/commit/36c75dc">open recovery commit ↗</a>
      </div>

      <div class="incident-black-case">
        <span>REGRESSION BLACK CASE</span>
        <p>
          canonical STATUS → <b>statusBackend</b><br />
          alias LOGIN → <b>normal backend</b><br />
          fallback → must not overwrite <b>statusBackend</b>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="evidence-grid evidence-grid-router">
      <div class="evidence-record evidence-record-observed">
        <span class="record-type">PROTOCOL BOUNDARY</span>
        <code>Java handshake → opaque TCP</code>
        <strong>Parse only enough protocol to choose a route.</strong>
        <p>
          The normal Java path inspects the initial handshake, forwards it unchanged, then stops understanding gameplay
          traffic.
        </p>
        <small>routing boundary</small>
      </div>

      <div class="evidence-record evidence-record-observed">
        <span class="record-type">DISCOVERY BOUNDARY</span>
        <code>providers → route snapshot</code>
        <strong>Discovery updates configuration; it does not invent routing semantics.</strong>
        <p>
          Static YAML and Kubernetes Service discovery feed the same immutable route model, with last-known-good behavior
          during discovery failures.
        </p>
        <small>runtime route model</small>
      </div>

      <div class="evidence-record evidence-record-observed">
        <span class="record-type">CONTROL PLANE</span>
        <code>monitoring / scaler / companions</code>
        <strong>Optional control features remain outside core routing correctness.</strong>
        <p>
          Monitoring can describe availability, a scaler can request lifecycle work and protocol companions can add state,
          but none silently redefine the Java routing contract.
        </p>
        <small>responsibility invariant</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="evidence-system evidence-reveal" id="evidence-platform">
    <header class="evidence-system-header">
      <div>
        <span class="evidence-kind">INFRASTRUCTURE / OPERATION</span>
        <h3>Home Kubernetes Platform</h3>
      </div>
      <p>There is no public repository to point at here. The evidence is the topology and the operational capability it enables.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="runtime-topology" aria-label="Current home platform topology">
      <span>PHYSICAL FAILURE DOMAIN</span>
      <div class="runtime-host">
        <span>HOST</span>
        <strong>1 physical server</strong>
        <small>current failure domain</small>
      </div>
      <div class="runtime-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="runtime-layer">
        <span>VIRTUALIZATION</span>
        <strong>Proxmox VE</strong>
        <small>VM lifecycle boundary</small>
      </div>
      <div class="runtime-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="runtime-vms">
        <div>
          <span>CONTROL PLANE VM</span>
          <strong>cp-01</strong>
          <small>cluster control / etcd</small>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>WORKER VM</span>
          <strong>worker-01</strong>
          <small>application workloads</small>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="runtime-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="runtime-layer runtime-kubernetes">
        <span>PLATFORM</span>
        <strong>Kubernetes</strong>
        <small>reconciliation boundary</small>
        <div class="runtime-services" aria-label="Platform responsibilities">
          <i>Cilium</i><i>Longhorn</i><i>Argo CD</i><i>CRI-O</i>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="operation-ledger">
      <section>
        <span>REBUILDABILITY</span>
        <strong>From guided install to repeatable self-rebuild.</strong>
        <p>
          The platform became understood well enough to rebuild independently and turn the process into installation
          guidance for other environments.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <span>REAL WORKLOADS</span>
        <strong>Minecraft and web services share one operating model.</strong>
        <p>
          Stateful game servers, network-edge software and conventional web workloads use the same reconciliation,
          storage, networking and delivery primitives.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <span>LIMITATION</span>
        <strong>Logical separation is not physical availability.</strong>
        <p>
          cp-01 and worker-01 are separate VMs, but one physical host still owns the failure domain. The topology is
          designed to expand without presenting the current machine as an HA cluster.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="evidence-principle evidence-reveal">
    <span>WHAT THIS LAYER PROVES</span>
    <p>
      The projects are not architecture diagrams assembled after the fact. Their invariants show up in persisted state,
      executable failure cases, recovery commits and infrastructure I have to keep operating.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="evidence-next evidence-reveal">
    <span>04 / live runtime</span>
    <i></i>
    <span>the running portfolio process becomes evidence next</span>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import '../scripts/evidence-operation.ts';
</script>